{
 "lead": "Passband modulation puts bits onto a sine-wave carrier so they can travel through air, cable or fibre. Binary amplitude shift keying (ASK) changes the carrier amplitude for the two bits; binary frequency shift keying (BFSK) keeps the amplitude and switches between two frequencies. Both are cheap, so they run car key fobs, RFID tags, 433 MHz remotes and low-rate telemetry.",
 "prereq": "You need: the correlator (matched filter) receiver and signal-space points (see baseband data transmission and detection), and white Gaussian noise of two-sided density N<sub>0</sub>/2 (see signals, noise and random processes).",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Binary ASK and on-off keying",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Binary ASK sends the carrier at one amplitude for bit 1 and another for bit 0. When the second amplitude is zero it is on-off keying (OOK): bit 1 is a carrier burst, bit 0 is silence. Exams usually mean OOK by ASK."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "s(t) = A·b(t)·cos(2πf<sub>c</sub> t)",
     "where": "A is the amplitude in volts, b(t) is 1 or 0 during the bit, f<sub>c</sub> is the carrier frequency in Hz and T<sub>b</sub> the bit time in s; the bit rate is R<sub>b</sub> = 1/T<sub>b</sub>."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>Generation.</b> Multiply unipolar NRZ data (level 1 or 0) by the carrier in a product modulator, or simply switch the carrier on and off."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>Coherent detection.</b> Multiply by a phase-locked local carrier 2cos(2πf<sub>c</sub> t), integrate over one bit and sample at T<sub>b</sub>. Without noise the output is A·T<sub>b</sub> for a 1 and 0 for a 0, so the threshold is A·T<sub>b</sub>/2."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>Energy</b> (1 Ω reference). A burst has energy A²T<sub>b</sub>/2. With equally likely bits the average is E<sub>b</sub> = A²T<sub>b</sub>/4 and the average power A²/4, half the peak A²/2. The signal-space points are 0 and √(2E<sub>b</sub>)."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "BFSK: two tones",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "BFSK sends bit 1 at the mark frequency f<sub>1</sub> and bit 0 at the space frequency f<sub>0</sub>, both at amplitude A, so every bit has energy E<sub>b</sub> = A²T<sub>b</sub>/2."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>Generation.</b> A voltage-controlled oscillator (VCO) driven by the data swings between f<sub>0</sub> and f<sub>1</sub> with continuous phase (the root of <a href=\"#msk-and-gmsk\">MSK</a>). Switching between two oscillators also works, but the phase jumps widen the spectrum."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>Coherent detection.</b> Two correlators, at f<sub>1</sub> and at f<sub>0</sub>, each integrate over T<sub>b</sub> and the larger output wins. In signal space the points are (√E<sub>b</sub>, 0) and (0, √E<sub>b</sub>), √(2E<sub>b</sub>) apart, with the 45° line between them as the boundary."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>Orthogonal tones.</b> Each correlator ignores the other tone only if the tones are orthogonal over a bit. Coherent detection needs |f<sub>1</sub> − f<sub>0</sub>| = nR<sub>b</sub>/2 with n a positive whole number (carriers well above R<sub>b</sub>); the smallest is R<sub>b</sub>/2. Non-coherent detection needs nR<sub>b</sub>, at least R<sub>b</sub> (see <a href=\"#non-coherent-detection-and-dpsk\">non-coherent detection</a>). Being above the minimum is not enough."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>Δf = |f<sub>1</sub> − f<sub>0</sub>|/2,  h = 2Δf/R<sub>b</sub></b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Δf is the deviation from the centre (f<sub>1</sub> + f<sub>0</sub>)/2 and h is the modulation index."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "B ≈ 2(Δf + R<sub>b</sub>)",
     "where": "Carson's rule for BFSK, with R<sub>b</sub> standing in for the baseband bandwidth of the data."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Spectra and error probability",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The OOK spectrum is a sinc² lobe on f<sub>c</sub> plus a carrier line (the unipolar data has a non-zero mean); its nulls at f<sub>c</sub> ± R<sub>b</sub> give a null-to-null bandwidth of 2R<sub>b</sub>. BFSK looks like two such lobes, one per tone."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "In AWGN of two-sided density N<sub>0</sub>/2, two signal points a distance d apart give P<sub>b</sub> = Q(d/√(2N<sub>0</sub>)), where Q(x) is the normal tail area beyond x. Both schemes have d = √(2E<sub>b</sub>), so"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>P<sub>b</sub> = Q(√(E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub>))</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "for coherent OOK (E<sub>b</sub> = A²T<sub>b</sub>/4, the average) and for coherent orthogonal BFSK (E<sub>b</sub> = A²T<sub>b</sub>/2). N<sub>0</sub> is the one-sided noise density in W/Hz."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Both are 3 dB worse than <a href=\"#bpsk-generation-detection-and-error-probability\">BPSK</a>, whose points are 2√E<sub>b</sub> apart. At the same peak amplitude BFSK has twice the E<sub>b</sub> of OOK. BFSK also has a constant envelope, so a saturating amplifier is fine, while the OOK threshold depends on the received amplitude and moves when the signal fades."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "A BFSK transmitter sends R<sub>b</sub> = 10 kb/s with a 30 kHz mark tone (bit 1) and a 20 kHz space tone (bit 0). Find Δf, h and the Carson bandwidth. Are the tones orthogonal for coherent and for non-coherent detection? What changes with a 27.5 kHz mark tone?",
   "steps": [
    "T<sub>b</sub> = 1/R<sub>b</sub> = 100 µs, so the mark tone makes 3 cycles per bit and the space tone 2.",
    "Spacing |f<sub>1</sub> − f<sub>0</sub>| = 10 kHz, so Δf = 5 kHz around a centre of 25 kHz.",
    "h = 2Δf/R<sub>b</sub> = 10/10 = 1.",
    "B = 2(Δf + R<sub>b</sub>) = 2(5 + 10) = 30 kHz.",
    "Coherent test: R<sub>b</sub>/2 = 5 kHz and 10 = 2 × 5, so n = 2: orthogonal.",
    "Non-coherent test: R<sub>b</sub> = 10 kHz and 10 = 1 × 10: orthogonal too.",
    "At 27.5 kHz the spacing is 7.5 kHz = 1.5 × (R<sub>b</sub>/2): above the minimum but not a whole multiple. The tones' correlation over a bit is about −0.25 of the full value, so each correlator picks up a quarter of the wrong tone."
   ],
   "answer": "Δf = 5 kHz, h = 1, B = 30 kHz; the 10 kHz spacing is orthogonal for both receivers. A 7.5 kHz spacing is not orthogonal although it exceeds R<sub>b</sub>/2."
  },
  {
   "question": "OOK and BFSK links both use peak amplitude A = 10 mV (1 Ω), R<sub>b</sub> = 10 kb/s and N<sub>0</sub> = 2.5 × 10<sup>−10</sup> W/Hz. Find the peak power and, for each, E<sub>b</sub>, E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub> and the coherent P<sub>b</sub>.",
   "steps": [
    "Peak power A²/2 = (0.01)²/2 = 50 µW for both; T<sub>b</sub> = 100 µs.",
    "OOK: E<sub>b</sub> = A²T<sub>b</sub>/4 = 2.5 × 10<sup>−9</sup> J, so E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub> = 10 (10 dB) and P<sub>b</sub> = Q(√10) = Q(3.162) = 7.83 × 10<sup>−4.</sup>",
    "BFSK: E<sub>b</sub> = A²T<sub>b</sub>/2 = 5 × 10<sup>−9</sup> J, so E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub> = 20 (13 dB) and P<sub>b</sub> = Q(√20) = Q(4.472) = 3.87 × 10<sup>−6.</sup>",
    "Check: OOK is silent half the time, so its E<sub>b</sub> is half that of BFSK; those 3 dB cost a factor of about 200 in P<sub>b</sub>."
   ],
   "answer": "Peak power 50 µW. OOK: 2.5 nJ, 10 dB, P<sub>b</sub> = 7.83 × 10<sup>−4.</sup> BFSK: 5 nJ, 13 dB, P<sub>b</sub> = 3.87 × 10<sup>−6.</sup>"
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "Δf is half the tone spacing: for 30 kHz and 20 kHz tones Δf = 5 kHz, not 10 kHz, and h = 1.",
  "Orthogonality needs a whole multiple: nR<sub>b</sub>/2 for coherent and nR<sub>b</sub> for non-coherent detection. 7.5 kHz at 10 kb/s is above the minimum and still not orthogonal.",
  "In B = 2(Δf + R<sub>b</sub>), R<sub>b</sub> is the bit rate in bit/s; putting in T<sub>b</sub> gives nonsense.",
  "For OOK, E<sub>b</sub> is the average A²T<sub>b</sub>/4. Using the burst energy A²T<sub>b</sub>/2 makes OOK look 3 dB better than it is.",
  "Coherent detection needs a phase-locked carrier; an envelope detector is non-coherent and has other formulas."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "What is on-off keying, and how is it related to binary ASK?",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "OOK is binary ASK whose bit-0 amplitude is zero: a carrier burst for 1, silence for 0."
  },
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "Write the BFSK modulation index and Carson bandwidth.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "h = 2Δf/R<sub>b</sub> = |f<sub>1</sub> − f<sub>0</sub>|/R<sub>b</sub> and B ≈ 2(Δf + R<sub>b</sub>)."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why is the coherent OOK threshold A·T<sub>b</sub>/2?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "The correlator gives A·T<sub>b</sub> for 1 and 0 for 0; with equally likely bits and symmetric noise the midpoint minimises errors."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "BFSK at 20 kb/s uses tones at 200 kHz and 210 kHz. Find Δf, h and B, and test coherent orthogonality.",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "Δf = 5 kHz, h = 0.5, B = 2(5 + 20) = 50 kHz. R<sub>b</sub>/2 = 10 kHz, so n = 1: coherently orthogonal (the MSK spacing)."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "Coherent BFSK runs at E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub> = 12 dB. Find P<sub>b</sub>.",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "12 dB = 15.85; P<sub>b</sub> = Q(√15.85) = Q(3.981) = 3.43 × 10<sup>−5.</sup>"
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "Why does BFSK normally need more bandwidth than OOK at the same bit rate?",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "OOK fills one lobe, 2R<sub>b</sub> wide. BFSK adds the tone spacing to that, and the spacing cannot go below R<sub>b</sub>/2 without losing orthogonality."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "Fading halves the received amplitude. Which suffers more with a fixed threshold, coherent OOK or BFSK?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "OOK: its threshold was set for the old amplitude, so 1s now sit on it. BFSK compares two outputs that scale together."
  }
 ],
 "beyond": "Not for the exam. OOK still runs most short optical links, where a laser is switched on and off, and the 433 MHz remote-control band. Bluetooth Low Energy and many sensor radios use GFSK, a filtered continuous-phase FSK. Software radios now detect FSK by measuring instantaneous frequency in software. Search next: continuous-phase FSK, GFSK, frequency discriminator.",
 "figure": {
  "after_section": 1,
  "caption": "The worked example’s bits 1 0 1 1 0 at 10 kb/s. OOK sends 3 cycles of a 30 kHz carrier for each 1 and nothing for each 0; BFSK sends 3 cycles (30 kHz) for a 1 and 2 cycles (20 kHz) for a 0, so its envelope never changes.",
